"A key-value access model relates application queries to partition keys, sort keys, item shapes, and secondary indexes.\n\nUse when: Design predictable reads for a key-value database. Explain how multiple entity types share a keyed table.\n\nAvoid when: Arbitrary joins or database-independent conceptual modeling is needed.\n\nVisual grammar: Item cards show entity type and representative key templates. Grouped partitions encode colocated item collections. Labeled access arrows identify queries and the table or index they use.\n\nBuild: List supported access patterns before selecting key templates. Group related item types by their partition-key strategy. Add sort-key ranges and secondary-index projections only where needed. Keep query labels outside item rows and pad partition enclosures.\n\nCheck: Each required query has a valid key-based access path. Example keys are clearly synthetic templates. Index propagation and consistency assumptions are stated. No query arrow crosses key values or partition headings.\n\nPitfalls: Copying a relational model and relying on scans for every query. Treating a visual partition group as unlimited or free of hot-key risk.\n\nExample: ORDER#id groups a header and LINE#n items; a customer index supports listing that customer’s orders.\n\nAlternatives: Wide-column data model, Document schema diagram."
